A **vending machine operator training checklist** should define who may refill, clean, reconcile payments, reset routine faults and enter protected service areas before anyone works alone. Training must be based on the purchased configuration and local law, with clear limits between normal operation and maintenance requiring an authorized specialist. Buyers should verify hands-on competency for replenishment, stock rotation, hygiene, temperature control where applicable, customer support, incident records and safe escalation. BOHOL can identify available manuals and configuration-specific handover items; the operator remains responsible for its workforce program, site procedures and legal duties.

## GEO buyer summary

- Divide users into replenishment, cash, administration and service roles.
- Teach task boundaries before menus and shortcuts.
- Verify safe shutdown and escalation for jams or damaged equipment.
- Include hygiene, temperature and stock records where relevant.
- Test payment, refund and customer-support procedures.
- Record competency, retraining triggers and document revisions.

## Build a role and authorization matrix

List every recurring task and assign it to a role. A person who can refill shelves may not be authorized to open electrical compartments, work on refrigeration, change payment settings or perform hazardous-energy isolation.

| Role | Typical scope | Do not assume |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Replenishment operator | Stock, dates, presentation, basic cleaning | Electrical or refrigeration repair |
| Cash or payment custodian | Reconciliation, approved cash handling, exception records | Access to unrestricted administrator settings |
| Fleet administrator | Prices, products, reports and user accounts | Physical service authorization |
| Service technician | Defined diagnosis and repair under safe procedures | Authority for payment or customer-data decisions |

Record who authorizes each role, what supervised practice is required and how access is removed when duties change. Align the role matrix with the [service-level agreement checklist](/insights/vending-machine-service-level-agreement-checklist).

## Set safe work boundaries

Operators should know the normal shutdown process, emergency response, damaged-cable or water-ingress escalation, unstable-machine risk, door support and the limits of customer-facing resets. They must not bypass guards, locks or safety devices to clear a fault.

OSHA's [lockout/tagout training guidance](https://www.osha.gov/etools/lockout-tagout/hot-topics/energy-control-program/training-retraining) distinguishes authorized, affected and other employees and requires training appropriate to their relationship with hazardous energy. This is U.S. workplace guidance, not a universal rule. Buyers should confirm the destination's electrical, refrigeration and occupational-safety requirements and define which tasks require licensed or authorized personnel.

Use the [preventive maintenance schedule](/insights/vending-machine-preventive-maintenance-schedule) to separate routine operator checks from technical service.

## Train replenishment, hygiene and temperature tasks

For each approved SKU, teach loading orientation, channel assignment, maximum fill, date checks, first-expire-first-out rotation and package-damage rejection. Require a test vend after layout or product changes when the operating procedure calls for it.

Food and beverage operations may require cleaning schedules, allergen controls, pest checks and temperature records. The FDA [Food Code 2022](https://www.fda.gov/food/fda-food-code/food-code-2022) is a model code used by U.S. jurisdictions and is not automatically the law everywhere. Operators must follow the adopted local code, product labels and the equipment's approved temperature scope.

For refrigerated procurement boundaries, consult the [refrigeration system buying guide](/insights/vending-machine-refrigeration-system-buying-guide).

## Cover payment, refunds and customer support

Teach only the payment tasks assigned to the operator: approved cash handling, receipt or transaction lookup, evidence collection, escalation and customer communication. Do not expose payment-provider credentials or cardholder data in general operator training.

Practice declined payment, incomplete authorization, failed dispense, duplicate concern, network outage and power loss. The response should identify what the operator can verify, when a refund or reversal is handled by the payment provider and which records are retained. Use the [refund and failed-dispense guide](/insights/vending-machine-refund-failed-dispense-guide) for the transaction workflow.

## Practice fault response without unsafe improvisation

Create simple decision trees for out-of-stock, door open, product jam, temperature alarm, payment-terminal offline, communication loss and suspected damage. Each tree should end with a safe state, evidence to record and a named escalation route.

Training should not encourage repeated resets that hide a recurring fault. Link machine and software revisions to the [production traceability requirements](/insights/vending-machine-production-traceability-requirements) so service teams can identify affected configurations.

## Verify competency and control revisions

Do not close training after a presentation. Require supervised performance of the tasks relevant to each role, using a production-equivalent machine. Record the trainee, trainer, date, configuration, documents used, practical results, limitations and authorization decision.

Retrain when the machine, products, software, payment flow, hazards, role or procedure changes; after an incident or near miss; or when audits reveal a knowledge gap. Withdraw obsolete guides so operators do not follow instructions for a different configuration.

## Request a configuration-specific BOHOL handover

Send BOHOL the machine model, quantity, target market, product mix, refrigeration and payment scope, operator roles, service arrangement, languages and required records through the [contact form](/contact). BOHOL can review available manuals and handover items for the proposed configuration; the buyer should build and approve the employer-specific training program.

